tags: Examples
stages:
  press the asterisk -->:
    remark: |-
      The Green asterisk is a way to read remarks written by the Broadway flow developer.

      Select the asterisk in the main menu to popup all remarks in the flow.

      You can add remarks from the ... context menus.
  Stage 1:
    remark: "A flow is built out of stages. The stages are executed one by one from\
      \ left to right.\nTo run a flow, select the Play option in the main menu. \n\
      You can put a break point at any stage from its ... context menu or by clicking\
      \ next to its name.\nYou can run the flow stage by stage by using the Step icon.\n"
    transactional: false
  Hello Broadway:
    remark: "A stage contains Actors. \n\nAn actor is an Object with a function that\
      \ gets input parameters and returns output parameters. \n\nWhen debugging, you\
      \ can see the input and  output parameters of each actor.\n"
    transactional: false
    actors:
      Const1:
        parent: Const
        in:
          value:
            const: Hello Broadway
            schema: string
        out:
          value:
            schema: string
  Input Arguments:
    remark: |-
      Input arguments can get their value from several sources:

      1) A constant value (as can be seen in the previous stage).
      2) The output of a previous actor.
      3) An external flow argument

      Select the input method by clicking the actor to open the actor’s Properties Pane and selecting the ... context menu of the input parameter under the inputs section.
    transactional: false
    actors:
      Const2:
        parent: Const
        in:
          value:
            const: null
            link: Const1/value
            schema: string
        out:
          value:
            schema: string
  strings:
    remark: |-
      Broadway contains several actors to deal with string parsing and formatting.

      Checkout the strings.flow example for details on String functions.
    transactional: false
    actors:
      Split1:
        parent: Split
        in:
          delimiter:
            const: ' '
          string:
            link: Const2/value
  for each:
    remark: |-
      Often we have cases where we want to deal with individual elements in a data set.
      Broadway supports that with the "Iterate" connection type. You can change the line type by selecting the line and changing its “Line Type” value to “Iterate” in the Properties Pane.

      To close the scope of the iteration, select "Iterate Close" from the ... stage context menu.

      Inside the loop we have the StringBuild actor that accumulates the result.

      For more on the subject, checkout the iterate-for-each.flow example.
    last: 1
    transactional: false
    actors:
      StringBuild1:
        parent: StringBuild
        remark: |-
          An actor can have a state.

          Here we see the StringBuild actor that maintains a state (the string so far) between iterations.
        in:
          delimiter:
            const: '*'
          input:
            link:
              path: Split1/strings
              iterate: Iterate
  primitives:
    remark: |-
      Broadway supports a closed list of primitive types.

      When an actor expects a certain primitive type, Broadway does its best to convert the instance to the desired type.
    transactional: false
    actors:
      aString:
        parent: Replace
        in:
          search:
            const: '*'
          replace:
            const: ' '
          string:
            link: StringBuild1/string
      anInteger:
        parent: Const
        remark: |+
          Integer is implemented using a signed 64 bit container (Java long).


        in:
          value:
            const: 42
            schema: integer
        out:
          value:
            schema: integer
      aReal:
        parent: Const
        remark: "Real is a floating point double precision variable implemented by\
          \ the Java double type.\nIt can receive edge values such as Infinite and\
          \ NaN.\n\nCheckout the math.flow example to see it in action along with\
          \ the many builtin math actors that are included in Broadway. "
        in:
          value:
            const: 3.14
            schema: number
        out:
          value:
            schema: number
      aDate:
        parent: Const
        remark: "Dates signal a point in time. They are implemented by the java Date\
          \ class and its derivatives (sql.Timestamp, sql.DateTime, etc).\n\nBroadway\
          \ has a comprehensive list of builtin actors to deal with dates and time\
          \ zones.\n \nCheck out the dates.flow example for more."
        in:
          value:
            const: 1623-06-19T23:32
            schema: date
        out:
          value:
            schema: date
      aBoolean:
        parent: Const
        remark: |-
          Yes/No, True/False, 0/1.

          All data types have some default conversion  to the boolean type.

          For more, see the logic.flow example.
        in:
          value:
            const: true
            schema: boolean
        out:
          value:
            schema: boolean
  splitting the flow:
    remark: |-
      You can split the flow and assign a condition to each sub stage.
      An 'else' stage will run if all other stage conditions returned false.

      The stages in the same row are dependent on the stages before them.

      You can split the stages indefinitely to create a large condition tree. However, it would be a better practice to use an inner flow instead of a large number of splits.

      To see more check out the conditions.flow  example.
    else: true
    transactional: false
    dependsOn: primitives
    actors:
      Logger1:
        parent: Logger
        remark: 'Use the Logger actor to format and send messages to the log file. '
        in:
          message:
            const: All is well with the world
          level:
            const: info
    split: '--------------------'
  paradox:
    transactional: false
    dependsOn: primitives
    actors:
      never:
        parent: JavaScript
        condition: result
        in:
          script:
            const: true == !true
        out:
          result:
            schema: boolean
      Logger2:
        parent: Logger
        in:
          message:
            const: Universe is broken
  Dynamic Logic Actors:
    remark: "Some actors have dynamic logic as one of their inputs. \nFor these actors\
      \ you can add inputs ports and refer to them in the Actor logic."
    dependsOn: splitting the flow
    actors:
      JavaScript1:
        parent: JavaScript
        remark: You can see the JS Actor in action in the javascript.flow
        in:
          script:
            const: |-
              let gp= input1.split(" ");
              // The last statement is the return value:
              ({
                  data:{
                      "greeting": gp[0],
                      "product": gp[1]
                  }
              });
          input1:
            link: aString/string
            schema: string
        out:
          result:
            schema: '#ref'
      StringFormat1:
        parent: StringFormat
        remark: StringFormat is a surprisingly powerful actor. Select the "Description"
          entry from the Actor ... context menu to learn more.
        in:
          format:
            const: ${input1} != ${input2}
          params:
            const: ''
          input1:
            link: anInteger/value
            schema: integer
          input2:
            link: aReal/value
            schema: number
      DbCommand1:
        parent: DbCommand
        remark: |
          This is a simple SQL. You can see more complex usage of the DbCommand actor in the db_commands.flow and db_insert.flow
        in:
          interface:
            const: 'jdbc:sqlite:'
          sql:
            const: "SELECT \"Broadway Rocks\" as \ncompliment\nWHERE ${input1}=TRUE"
          input1:
            link: aBoolean/value
            schema: boolean
        out:
          result:
            schema: '#ref'
    split: '--------------------'
  Stage 3:
    dependsOn: paradox
  Complex data:
    remark: |-
      Broadway can work with complex data types (arrays / maps) and enable you to pick data directly from these structures.

      Click the + on the result output parameter of JavaScript1 and DbCommand1 to see the metadata.

      You can also edit the meta data directly on the meta data viewer by clicking the fields
    transactional: false
    actors:
      Concat1:
        parent: Concat
        remark: We pass the data map as an input to the Concat actor. Since concat
          expects an Array, Broadway will automatically pass the map values to the
          Concat actor
        in:
          delimiter:
            const: '-'
          elements:
            link: JavaScript1/result/data
      StringFormat2:
        parent: StringFormat
        remark: Taking this opportunity to build a simple json to showcase a parser
        in:
          format:
            const: '{"${input1}":"${input2}"}'
          input1:
            link: JavaScript1/result/data/greeting
            schema: string
          input2:
            link: JavaScript1/result/data/product
            schema: string
      Concat2:
        parent: Concat
        remark: |-
          In a situation where an Actor expects an array you can build the array on the fly from discrete  entries.

          To use this mode, click the line to bring the line inspector and toggle the Variable Arguments (varargs) selector.
        in:
          delimiter:
            const: '-'
          elements:
            link:
            - path: JavaScript1/result/data/greeting
              pos: 0
            - path: JavaScript1/result/data/product
              pos: 1
  Parsers:
    remark: "Parsers take a string or blob and parse them into a structure.\n\nInput\
      \ can also be a collection of blobs or strings which is how Broadway represents\
      \ a data stream.   \n\nCheckout json.flow, csv.flow and lines.flow from more\
      \ examples as well as the actor documentation (context menu > description)."
    transactional: false
    actors:
      CsvParser1:
        parent: CsvParser
        remark: When a header row is present, the CSV actor will return named rows
          and Broadway's schema discovery engine will analyse them.
        in:
          stream:
            const: |-
              Greeting,Product
              Hello,Broadway
              Goodbye,PHP
        out:
          object:
            schema: '#ref'
      JsonParser1:
        parent: JsonParser
        in:
          stream:
            link: StringFormat2/string
        out:
          object:
            schema: '#ref'
      LinesParser1:
        parent: LinesParser
        remark: The const here shows how data can come in different chunks with arbitrary
          alignment and not confuse our parsers.
        in:
          stream:
            const:
            - li
            - |-
              ne1
              l
            - ine2
      XmlParser1:
        parent: XmlParser
        in:
          stream:
            const: |-
              <root>
                <a>1</a>
                <b attr="2.5">2</b>
                <c>3</c>
              </root>
            schema: string
        out:
          object:
            schema: '#ref'
          info:
            schema: '#ref'
  more js + external:
    remark: "Understanding the Broadway API and the JS actor can open up some very\
      \ powerful options.\n\nCheck out the javascript-advanced.flow for dynamic flowArgs,\
      \ error handling and more.\n\nWhen marking an output parameter external, it\
      \ becomes an output for the entire flow.\nYou can see input and output parameters\
      \ in action in the javascript-advanced.flow and the inner-flow-power.flow "
    transactional: false
    actors:
      JavaScript2:
        parent: JavaScript
        in:
          script:
            const: |-
              let r = "";
              for (let i in input1) {
                  r+= i + " " + input1[i];
              }
              r;
          input1:
            link: JsonParser1/object
            schema: '#ref'
        out:
          result:
            external: result
            schema: string
  streams:
    remark: "Broadway has some powerful built in actors to deal with Input and Output\
      \ streams as well as file system interaction. \n\nCheckout files.flow, http.flow,\
      \ http-error.flow  "
    transactional: false
    dependsOn: more js + external
    actors:
      HttpDemoServer1:
        parent: HttpDemoServer
      Http1:
        parent: Http
        in:
          interface:
            const: http://localhost:8841
          path:
            const: /
        out:
          header:
            schema: '#ref'
    split: '--------------------'
  Stage 6:
    dependsOn: more js + external
    split: '--------------------'
  publish:
    remark: |-
      Message Broker integration is available using the Publish/Subscribe actors.
      You can use them for simple in memory communication or integration with Kafka/Pulsar/JMS etc.

      See more details in message-broker-pubsub.example
    transactional: false
    dependsOn: more js + external
    actors:
      Publish1:
        parent: Publish
        in:
          interface:
            const: broker:memory
          topic:
            const: ExampleTopic
          message:
            const: Hello Broadway
    split: '--------------------'
  Stage 2:
    transactional: false
    dependsOn: more js + external
    actors:
      Sort1:
        parent: Sort
        remark: Check out the sort.flow example for different ways to use the Sort
          actor
        in:
          input:
            const:
            - 1
            - 3
            - 2
        out:
          list:
            schema: '#ref'
      Lookup1:
        parent: Lookup
        remark: 'Check out lookup.flow actor for advanced use of the Lookup actor '
        in:
          lookupKeys:
            const:
            - a
          lookupData:
            const:
            - a: Hello
              b: Broadway
            schema: '#ref'
          a:
            const: Hello
            schema: string
        out:
          result:
            schema: '#ref'
  stringify the stream:
    dependsOn: streams
    actors:
      Concat3:
        parent: Concat
        in:
          elements:
            link: Http1/stream
    split: '--------------------'
  Stage 7:
    dependsOn: Stage 6
    split: '--------------------'
  subscribe:
    dependsOn: publish
    actors:
      Subscribe1:
        parent: Subscribe
        in:
          interface:
            const: broker:memory
          topic:
            const: ExampleTopic
          poll_timeout:
            const: 0
        out:
          message:
            schema: '#ref'
    split: '--------------------'
  Stage 4:
    transactional: false
    dependsOn: Stage 2
schemas:
  JavaScript1.out.result:
    type: object
    properties:
      data:
        type: object
        properties:
          greeting:
            type: string
          product:
            type: string
  DbCommand1.out.result:
    type: array
    items:
      type: object
      properties:
        compliment:
          type: string
  CsvParser1.out.object:
    type: array
    items:
      type: object
      properties:
        Greeting:
          type: string
        Product:
          type: string
  JsonParser1.out.object:
    type: object
    properties:
      Hello:
        type: string
  XmlParser1.out.object:
    type: object
    properties:
      root:
        type: object
        properties:
          a:
            type: string
          b#attr:
            type: string
          b:
            type: string
          c:
            type: string
  XmlParser1.out.info:
    type: object
    properties:
      version:
        type: string
      standalone:
        type: boolean
      header:
        type: string
  JavaScript2.in.input1:
    type: object
    properties:
      Hello:
        type: string
  Http1.out.header:
    type: object
    properties:
      Content-Length:
        type: integer
      Date:
        type: date
  Sort1.out.list:
    type: array
    items:
      type: number
  Lookup1.in.lookupData:
    type: array
    items:
      type: object
      properties:
        a:
          type: string
        b:
          type: string
  Lookup1.out.result:
    type: object
    properties:
      a:
        type: string
      b:
        type: string
  Subscribe1.out.message:
    type: array
    items:
      type: string
